l7a1045: the wave transfer trails the ADC instead of overtaking it

This is the crackle that survived 0055. Stereo sampling was correct at every
stage by then and still every take came back with a buzz on it, the input
meters twitched, and - the detail that turned out to name the fault - the
monitor sounded clean until RECORD was pressed.

TWO CHANNELS, ONE STREAM OF WORDS, TWO DIFFERENT SHAPES. A take moves through
the guest's 0x7F000 ring twice. DMA channel 2 writes the ADC into it; channel 3
reads it back out into wave RAM. The header already stated the invariant that
makes that work - "channel 3's Nth word is channel 2's Nth word" - but nothing
enforced it. Channel 2 is driven by data: update_adc_drq() holds DRQ while the
capture ring has words, so the DMAC empties it at bus speed. Channel 3 is
driven by a timer at the ADC rate. On the real machine both are paced by the
one 44.1kHz audio clock and the invariant is free. Here the ADC clock is the
sound stream, which hands over fifteen frames at a time, once every 333us. So
channel 2's write pointer stands still and then leaps thirty words, while
channel 3's advances evenly. Identical words per second, opposite shape.

They crossed on every block. Instrumenting the phase - (channel 2 words in)
minus (channel 3 words out), sampled at every wave word and reported twice a
second - measured it directly:

    PHASE block=15 depth_min=0 starved=0 held=0 lag=[-25,3]

Channel 3 ran up to twenty-six words AHEAD of channel 2 and spent most of every
333us copying ring slots the ADC had not filled yet, about three thousand times
a second. What sat in those slots was the previous lap of the 2KiB ring, so the
take was not merely late in places, it had stale audio spliced into it at the
block rate. That is audible as a buzz, not as clicks, and it is why it appears
on RECORD: while the machine is only metering, the firmware polls the DSP
constantly and every poll calls m_stream->update(), which splits production into
one- and two-frame blocks and leaves nothing to cross. The take stops the
polling, blocks go to their full fifteen frames, and the sawtooth appears.

The spectrum says the same thing without any reference to the emulator's
internals. The probe is a pure tone per channel, and both captures carry
sidebands at the tone plus and minus 3kHz - the sound-update block rate, and
nothing in the audio path has any other reason to produce it.

SO GATE THE TICK ON CHANNEL 2 BEING AHEAD. Channel 3 keeps its own even timer,
which is what makes the wave-RAM write pointer advance smoothly for the voice
monitoring the take and for the meter scan; it simply may not run until the
words it is about to copy exist. The lead is one sound-update block, taken from
the largest block the machine has actually handed over during this take rather
than chosen: one block is the whole amplitude of the sawtooth, so one block of
lead is what stops the gate biting again. Note the gate is a floor and not a
pace - any lead at all makes the crossing impossible, and the size only decides
whether channel 3 runs evenly or stutters.

MEASURED, A/B, ON ONE BINARY. Both arms are the same build with the same reset
of the audio graph before each; the switch that bypassed the gate existed only
for this comparison and is not in the diff. Phase, per half-second report:

                       before              after
    lag            [-25,+3]            [+29,+62]
    crossings      every block         none
    held           n/a                 55 in the first report, then 0
    starved        0                   0

"starved" is reads that found the capture ring empty. It is zero in both arms,
which settles a standing question: the held-last-value path in adc_dma_r16_cb
is unreachable, level DRQ really does stop the DMAC rather than let it invent
audio, and depth_min=0 is the level DRQ working as designed and not a fault.

Sideband power at the tone plus/minus k*3kHz, as a fraction of the tone:

                   L before   L after     R before   R after
    MONITOR           1.31%     0.00%       18.73%     0.00%
    RECORD            2.61%     0.00%       46.06%     0.00%
    PLAYBACK          1.44%     0.00%       26.82%     0.00%
    PAD               1.44%     0.00%       58.66%     0.01%

And the same result with no assumption about where the artefact sits - total
energy from 2kHz to 20kHz as a fraction of all energy:

                   L before   L after     R before   R after
    MONITOR           2.84%     0.12%       29.71%     0.27%
    RECORD            6.74%     0.24%       52.00%     0.46%
    PLAYBACK          4.29%     0.34%       40.62%     0.54%
    PAD               3.79%     0.57%       53.24%     1.01%

Stereo separation is unchanged - 18.2x to 31.4x across the stages in the
before arm, 11.5x to 33.8x across the stages of three separate after runs, on
a measure that varies that much run to run because the PAD stage is a single
one-shot and the window that catches it moves. The take length is unchanged too: the ADC
word count per run is identical, and the wave word count moves from twenty-six
ahead to twenty-nine behind, which is exactly the lead and a third of a
millisecond of extra take.

Two other repairs were considered and rejected on the same reading of the
phase, not benchmarked. Calling m_stream->update() from the timer would pull
production forward, but the timer runs at 88.2kHz and every call is a full
thirty-two voice update. Slaving channel 3 to channel 2 word for word enforces
the invariant exactly, and is wrong for the reason the free-running timer is
right: it would make the wave-RAM write pointer as bursty as channel 2, and
the voice reading the take back would then overtake it between blocks. The
rate arithmetic in
arm_dma_timer() was checked and is correct for stereo - 33.8688MHz/768 is 44100
exactly, so production and consumption match to the attosecond and a lead once
established never drifts away.
--- a/src/devices/sound/l7a1045_l6028_dsp_a.h
+++ b/src/devices/sound/l7a1045_l6028_dsp_a.h
@@ -141,6 +141,25 @@
 	u64             m_adc_words = 0;
 	u32             m_rec_peak = 0;
 	u64             m_rec_report_at = 0;
+	// PHASE, not just depth. An average rate that matches proves nothing: the
+	// two DMA channels can each move the right number of words per second and
+	// still cross each other inside every block. depth_min is how close the
+	// ADC ring came to running dry; starved counts the reads that found it
+	// dry; lag_min/lag_max bracket (channel 2 words in) - (channel 3 words
+	// out), which is how far channel 3 trails the audio it is copying. A
+	// lag that reaches zero means channel 3 read wave data channel 2 had not
+	// written yet.
+	u32             m_rec_depth_min = 0;
+	u32             m_rec_starved = 0;
+	u32             m_rec_held = 0;
+	int64_t         m_rec_lag_min = 0;
+	int64_t         m_rec_lag_max = 0;
+	// The largest sound-update block the machine has handed over during this
+	// take, in frames. Not a statistic: dma_timer_callback() takes the lead
+	// channel 3 must keep behind channel 2 from it, so the transfer is paced
+	// against the granularity the audio actually arrives at rather than
+	// against a number somebody picked.
+	u32             m_rec_block = 0;
 	// Register-0 reads since the take began. The take-length poll is the one
 	// piece of this protocol that cannot be inferred from the outside - it is
 	// a read, so it leaves no trace in memory - and the first few are the ones
